    {
      "id": "S3",
      "obj_id": "S-3",
      "kind": "setup",
      "provenance": "from-note",
      "nl": {
        "statement": "The Gaussian-kernel discrepancy layer on ratio laws. Let \\(k(a,b)=\\exp(-(a-b)^2)\\) on \\(\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\), with values in \\((0,1]\\) and \\(k(a,a)=1\\). For \\(j\\ne i\\) the population discrepancy is \\(D_{ji}=\\operatorname{MMD}_k(\\operatorname{Law}_{P^0}(R_i),\\operatorname{Law}_{P^j}(R_i))\\), the distance between the two kernel mean embeddings, and the second-moment contrast is \\(F_{ji}=\\mathbb E_{P^j}[R_i^2]-\\mathbb E_{P^0}[R_i^2]\\). Inside the model stratum, \\(\\mathcal V_{G,s,\\pi}\\) is the set of mechanisms with \\(F_{\\pi^{-1}(j)\\pi^{-1}(i)}\\ne0\\) on every edge \\(j\\to i\\) of \\(G\\), and \\(\\mathcal U_{G,s,\\pi}\\) is the set with \\(D_{ji}>0\\) whenever \\(\\pi(j)\\lessdot_G\\pi(i)\\). The observable directed graph \\(H_D\\) on interventional environment labels has arrow \\(j\\to i\\) exactly when \\(D_{ji}>0\\). — Round-4 correction (decision P-D9): the ENTIRE kernel layer of this S-block is LOCAL. Per decision P-D1 the mean embedding uses the note's own explicit feature map \\(\\Phi(r)=(\\sqrt{2^m/m!}e^{-r^2}r^m)_{m\\ge0}\\) into `lp (fun _ : ℕ => ℝ) 2` (Mathlib.Analysis.InnerProductSpace.l2Space, verified via lean_leansearch `Hilbert space of square summable sequences lp 2` → `lp.instInnerProductSpace`), giving \\(\\langle\\Phi(a),\\Phi(b)\\rangle=k(a,b)\\) and \\(\\|\\Phi(r)\\|=1\\) by the exponential series. F2 defines locally, in `Helpers/Kernel.lean`: (i) a `UnitNormFeatureMap` interface (a map into a real inner-product space with `‖Φ r‖ = 1` and `⟪Φ a, Φ b⟫ = k a b`), (ii) `meanEmbedding` as the Bochner integral of \\(\\Phi\\) against a law, and (iii) \\(D_{ji}\\) as the norm of the difference of two mean embeddings. `Causalean.ML.Kernel.RKHS` is NOT reused and is NOT imported: `bypass-justified` — `Causalean.ML.IsRKHS` (ML/Kernel/RKHS.lean:22, re-read 2026-09-06: a one-field `structure` whose sole field is `reproducing : ∀ f x, feval f x = inner ℝ f (representer x)`) supplies only the reproducing identity `f x = ⟪f, representer x⟫`; it carries no mean-embedding, no unit-norm feature bound, and no bounded-feature concentration interface, so it cannot realize the Gaussian-MMD empirical-mean bound and stating the concentration lemma `against` it would be a partial fit masquerading as reuse. Search trace: grep `IsRKHS|meanEmbedding|MMD|mmd|representer` over ../Causalean/ML/Kernel/RKHS.lean → only `IsRKHS.reproducing`; grep `IsRKHS|meanEmbedding|MMD` over ../Causalean and ../doc/API.md → `Causalean.ML.IsRKHS`, `rkhs_ball_empiricalRademacher_le`, `rademacherComplexity_l2_ball_le` (Rademacher-complexity bounds over an RKHS ball, not embeddings or two-sample discrepancies); Mathlib side lean_leansearch `Hilbert space of square summable sequences lp 2` → `lp.instInnerProductSpace` (reused), and lean_leansearch `kernel mean embedding maximum mean discrepancy of two measures` (run 2026-09-06) → only `ProbabilityTheory.Kernel.density`/`comapRight`/`withDensity` plumbing, i.e. no mean-embedding or MMD declaration exists in Mathlib. So the feature map, the embedding, and \\(D_{ji}\\) are define-local, build-inline, over verified Mathlib Hilbert substrate only. \\(H_D\\) is a plain `Fin n → Fin n → Prop`; its acyclicity is a CONCLUSION of `thm:exact-ratio-decoder`, so it is not packaged as a `Causalean.DAG` until that theorem supplies the witness.",
